WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.660
Require authenticated access at the cluster boundary and authorize clients for specific

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:06.130
models or service classes.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:09.975
Protect APIs with encryption in transit, rate limits, and network controls.

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:13.060
Segment user-facing gateways, management interfaces, storage,

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:16.930
and worker traffic so compromise of one component does not expose the entire control

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:17.330
plane.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.640
Store service credentials in a managed secrets system, rotate them,

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.960
and avoid embedding them in images or scripts.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.260
Patch operating systems, GPU drivers, runtimes, front ends,

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.140
and orchestration components through a controlled process.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:33.500
Remove one node from service, apply the change, test health and representative requests,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.100
and expand the rollout only when results are acceptable.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:40.380
Maintain rollback packages and configuration snapshots because a syntactically successful

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.160
update can still change model behavior,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:43.620
memory use, or throughput.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.550
Monitor from the request down to the hardware.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.635
Track availability, error rates, queue time, time to first token, generation rate,

00:00:49.635 --> 00:00:50.880
and end-to-end latency.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:56.395
Correlate those signals with GPU utilization, VRAM, CPU, RAM, disk, network, power,

00:00:56.395 --> 00:00:57.590
temperature, and throttling.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.990
Health checks should detect whether a process is merely running and whether it can

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.420
actually answer a model request.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.370
Alert on user impact and sustained resource conditions rather than every brief

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.140
fluctuation.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.258
Prepare for incidents.

00:01:08.258 --> 00:01:12.330
Preserve useful logs without retaining sensitive prompts longer than policy allows.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.810
Define how to isolate a node, revoke credentials, stop abusive traffic,

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.950
restore a model version, and communicate service degradation.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.640
Review cost and energy consumption alongside performance;

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.225
idle accelerators and overloaded cooling are operational signals.

00:01:25.225 --> 00:01:28.975
A secure cluster is one whose normal state is visible, whose changes are controlled,

00:01:28.975 --> 00:01:33.600
and whose operators can recover from failure without improvising access or data handling.
